# EXPECT: assigns enclosing name
#
# `local x is x + 1` — the declaration's own initializer reads the ENCLOSING x.
#
# THE POINT OF THIS FIXTURE is that this was the SECOND ordering bug in one
# exemption, and the fix for the first reintroduced it one position over. The
# dominance test scanned every statement BEFORE the declaration and not the
# declaration itself — but a declaration does not dominate a read inside itself:
# the right-hand side is evaluated against the enclosing binding, before the
# inner name exists.
#
#   VM   101       AOT  1       both build, both rc 0
#
# emitted as `long eig_x = 0; eig_x = eig_x + 1;` — the enclosing 100 is never
# captured, so the increment runs against a zero-initialised C local.
#
# The three fixtures in this family are one rule seen from three sides:
#   nested_conditional_local   a declaration inside `if` dominates nothing
#   nested_local_after_use     a declaration does not dominate what precedes it
#   nested_local_self_reference a declaration does not dominate ITSELF
#
# Written as three cases rather than one because each was found separately, and
# the first two were each believed to close the class when they landed.

define f(y) as:
    local x is 100
    define b(d) as:
        local x is x + 1
        return x
    local r is b of 5
    return r

print of (f of 0)
